</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section empirically evaluates our proposed ADaMoRE framework. We assess its core representation quality and generalization capabilities through unsupervised node classification (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Evaluation on Unsupervised Tasks ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and few-shot learning (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Evaluation on Few-Shot Learning Tasks ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Additionally, we conduct a comprehensive analysis of the framework’s core components, training stability, and computational efficiency in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Further supplementary results, including performance on a downstream node clustering task (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2. Evaluation on Node Clustering ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Analyses ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>) and a hyperparameter sensitivity analysis (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5. Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Analyses ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>), are provided in the appendix. All implementation details and model configurations are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2. Experimental Environment and Model Configurations ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Evaluation on Unsupervised Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection evaluates ADaMoRE’s effectiveness in learning comprehensive graph representations for unsupervised node classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We evaluate our method on 10 node classification benchmarks covering both homophilic and heterophilic graphs. Specifically, for graphs with homophily, we adopt three widely-used citation networks: Cora and Pubmed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al., 2008</a>)</cite>, and the large-scale Ogbn-Arxiv <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mikolov et al., 2013</a>)</cite>; one co-purchase network: Computers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2019</a>)</cite>; and one co-authorship network: CS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. For graphs with heterophily, we adopt three webpage datasets: Cornell, Texas, and Wisconsin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al., 2020</a>)</cite>; one Wikipedia-derived network: Roman-Empire <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Platonov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; and one synthetic dataset: Minesweeper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Platonov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The detailed statistics of these datasets are summarized in Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.1. Dataset Details ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1. Dataset Details ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare ADaMoRE against state-of-the-art unsupervised graph representation learning baselines. These encompass <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Auto-Encoder Methods</span> like MaskGAE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and GDAE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; popular <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Contrastive Learning Methods</span> such as DGI<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, GMI<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, MVGRL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hassani and Khasahmadi, 2020</a>)</cite>, GRACE<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>, GBT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bielak et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, BGRL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakoor et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DSSL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and SP-GCL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>; and recent <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive GNN Methods</span>, including GREET<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> and S3GCL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and Evaluation.</span>
Following standard protocols, node embeddings are first learned unsupervisedly. Subsequently, a linear classifier is trained on these frozen embeddings to evaluate representation quality. We report the average accuracy and standard deviation over ten runs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Results.</span>
The results in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.6. Training Objective ‣ 3. The ADaMoRE Framework ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrate the robust and superior performance of ADaMoRE, which consistently achieves state-of-the-art results across a diverse range of graph benchmarks, showcasing its strong generalization ability. Unlike baselines that are often confined to a single GNN encoder or a fixed set of filters, ADaMoRE’s core advantage stems from its principled approach to managing a diverse pool of heterogeneous experts in a fully unsupervised setting. Our backbone-residual design, stabilized by an information-theoretic diversity regularizer, successfully addresses the training instability inherent in naive heterogeneous MoE. This enables ADaMoRE to effectively leverage the distinct computational capabilities of various GNNs, leading to a more powerful and nuanced adaptation to the complex structural patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Evaluation on Few-Shot Learning Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection evaluates the performance of ADaMoRE on few-shot learning benchmarks. These challenging tasks serve as a rigorous test of our method’s generalization capabilities when adapting to new scenarios with minimal labeled data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We evaluate ADaMoRE on six few-shot learning benchmarks covering both node and graph classification tasks. Specifically, for few-shot node classification, we adopt one webpage network: Wisconsin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al., 2020</a>)</cite>; one Wikipedia-derived network: Squirrel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rozemberczki et al., 2021</a>)</cite>; and one citation network: Citeseer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. For few-shot graph classification, we adopt three standard benchmarks: one protein structure dataset, PROTEINS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borgwardt et al., 2005</a>)</cite>; one enzyme dataset, ENZYMES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; and one chemical compound dataset, BZR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rossi and Ahmed, 2015</a>)</cite>. The detailed statistics of these datasets are summarized in Table <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ C.1. Dataset Details ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1. Dataset Details ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table" aria-describedby="acmlabel4">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison on 1-shot node classification and graph classification tasks (mean accuracy % <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std. dev. over 100 tasks). Best and second-best results are in <span id="S4.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> and <span id="S4.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>, respectively.</span><span id="acmlabel4" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">A comprehensive merged table showing few-shot learning performance. It is split into two sections for node and graph classification. The table compares ADaMoRE against a wide range of baselines, including specific GMoE variants, on six key datasets. The proposed ADaMoRE model demonstrates superior or highly competitive performance across all tasks and datasets shown.</span></figcaption>
