</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We benchmark ADaMoRE against a comprehensive suite of state-of-the-art methods for few-shot learning. The baselines are categorized as follows: (1) <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end-to-end Supervised GNNs</span>, such as GCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kipf and Welling, 2017</a>)</cite> and H2GCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, trained directly on the few-shot labels; (2) <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Pre-training Models</span>, including GraphCL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and DSSL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; (3) <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Prompt Learning Models</span>, such as GraphPrompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> and ProNoG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>; and critically, (4) several <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GMoE</span> variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> to specifically evaluate our architectural contributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and Evaluation Protocol.</span>
We evaluate on both node and graph classification under a <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-shot setting, where only <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> labeled instances per class are used for adaptation. For evaluation, we follow the standard prototype-based protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>: class prototypes are computed from the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> labeled instances, and a test instance is assigned the label of its nearest prototype in the embedding space. In the main paper, we focus on the most challenging 1-shot setting (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>), while the results for settings with more shots are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3. Extended Few-shot Learning Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Analyses ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2. Evaluation on Few-Shot Learning Tasks ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> confirms the strong generalization ability of ADaMoRE in few-shot settings. Such robust results across both node and graph-level tasks underscore the effectiveness of the versatile and adaptive representations learned by ADaMoRE. This positions our framework as a potent and reliable solution for diverse graph-based tasks, particularly when operating under the constraints of limited supervision. Unlike other pre-training paradigms that may learn more generic embeddings, ADaMoRE’s design, which combines a stable backbone with a functionally specialized residual expert pool, produces richer representations that are particularly effective for rapid adaptation from minimal data. The superior performance against various GMoE variants further validates our architectural choices, demonstrating that the backbone-residual design is a more effective and principled approach for constructing a powerful unsupervised Graph MoE.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Ablation Studies and Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Studies.</span>
Ablation studies, presented in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies and Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, affirm the essential contribution of each component in the ADaMoRE framework. The results first underscore the efficacy of our core architectural innovations: removing the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Residual Experts</span> or training without the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity Regularizer</span> both lead to a clear performance drop, validating the necessity of the backbone-residual design and the principled specialization it enables. The adaptive mechanisms are also shown to be critical. Disabling the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">View Generator</span>, which forces both channels to process the same raw graph structure, or relying on a single-view pathway (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cohesive/Dispersive View Only</span>) results in a significant performance decline, highlighting the importance of providing distinct structural perspectives. Finally, replacing the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Fusion</span> with a static <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NaiveConcat</span> baseline is suboptimal, confirming the benefit of a fine-grained, node-specific fusion strategy. Furthermore, we demonstrate the framework’s robustness to imperfect guiding signals in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4. Robustness to Imperfect Guiding Weights ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Analyses ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>, where performance degrades gracefully even when significant noise is introduced.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation study on the core components of ADaMoRE (mean accuracy % <math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std. dev.).</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:88.2pt;vertical-align:-42.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-43.1pt,11.0pt) scale(0.800264948451747,0.800264948451747) ;">
<table id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Model Variant</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Cora</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Pubmed</td>
