<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical Foundations</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1. </span>Justification for Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0 (Sample Complexity Reduction via Backbone-Residual Architecture). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> (Sample Complexity Reduction)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a more formal theoretical rationale for Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0 (Sample Complexity Reduction via Backbone-Residual Architecture). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. We argue that our backbone-residual architecture is significantly more sample-efficient than a naive MoE, leading to improved training stability. Our analysis is grounded in statistical learning theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Preliminaries: Sample Complexity and Rademacher Complexity.</span>
The sample complexity of a learning problem, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the minimum number of samples <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> required to guarantee that a learned function <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> from a function class <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a true error within <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the optimal error in that class, with probability at least <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Generalization theory links sample complexity to the complexity of the function class <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, measured by the Rademacher complexity, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. The required sample size <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits a quadratic dependence on this complexity:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(\epsilon,\delta)\propto\frac{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F})^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(\epsilon,\delta)\propto\frac{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F})^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, reducing the effective Rademacher complexity of the function space is key to improving sample efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The High Sample Complexity of a Naive Heterogeneous MoE.</span>
As established in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 0 (Catastrophic Sample Complexity of Naive Heterogeneous MoE). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, a naive heterogeneous MoE operates within a vast function space, <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Rademacher complexity of this space, <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})</annotation></semantics></math>, is enormous due to the gated union of disparate function classes. According to Eq. <a href="#A1.E18" title="In A.1. Justification for Lemma (Sample Complexity Reduction) ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Foundations ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, searching this large space requires a prohibitively high number of samples, manifesting as training instability under a weak unsupervised signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Backbone-Residual Architecture as a Complexity Reduction Strategy.</span>
Our architecture mitigates this by decomposing the learning problem. We formalize our motivation with the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Assumption 1 (Structured Approximation Capability of the Backbone).</h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The oracle function <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed as <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=f^{*}_{struct}+f^{*}_{res}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=f^{*}_{struct}+f^{*}_{res}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{struct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{struct}</annotation></semantics></math> is the "structured" component highly correlated with principal graph structures. As motivated by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 2.3 (Efficacy of Guided Complementary Filtering as a Backbone). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, our backbone, operating in <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, is designed to effectively capture this component. We assume it can learn a high-quality approximation, <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{approx}\in\mathcal{F}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{approx}\in\mathcal{F}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the norm of the residual target <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}=f^{*}-f_{approx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}=f^{*}-f_{approx}</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly smaller than the norm of the original oracle function:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|R^{*}\|=\|f^{*}-f_{approx}\|\leq\eta\|f^{*}\|\quad\text{for some constant }\eta\ll 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for some constant </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|R^{*}\|=\|f^{*}-f_{approx}\|\leq\eta\|f^{*}\|\quad\text{for some constant }\eta\ll 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given this assumption, the learning task for the residual MoE component is no longer to learn the entire complex function <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, but to learn the much simpler residual function <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. While the full capacity of the residual space <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{res}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{res}</annotation></semantics></math> might be large, the learning algorithm is guided toward a target with a small norm. This confines the search to a much smaller <span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effective subspace</span>, <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{res,eff}=\{f_{r}\in\mathcal{F}_{res}\mid\|f_{r}\|\leq\eta\|f^{*}\|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{res,eff}=\{f_{r}\in\mathcal{F}_{res}\mid\|f_{r}\|\leq\eta\|f^{*}\|\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Standard results in learning theory show that the Rademacher complexity of a function class is proportional to the norm of the functions it contains. Therefore, we can relate the complexities of the effective search spaces:</p>
<table id="A1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\propto\eta\|f^{*}\|\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})\propto\|f^{*}\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\propto\eta\|f^{*}\|\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})\propto\|f^{*}\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>, it directly follows that the effective Rademacher complexity of the residual learning task is significantly lower:</p>
<table id="A1.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\ll\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\ll\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">This leads to the relationship between the sample complexities as stated in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0 (Sample Complexity Reduction via Backbone-Residual Architecture). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Let <math id="A1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> be the sample complexity for the residual component:</p>
<table id="A1.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)\leq C\cdot\left(\frac{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})}{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})}\right)^{2}N_{Naive}(\epsilon,\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)\leq C\cdot\left(\frac{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})}{\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})}\right)^{2}N_{Naive}(\epsilon,\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\ll\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{res,eff})\ll\mathcal{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{Naive-hetero})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)\ll N_{Naive}(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{Res}(\epsilon,\delta)\ll N_{Naive}(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. This significant reduction in sample complexity provides the rigorous theoretical explanation for why our backbone-residual architecture is more stable and efficient. It decomposes the learning task into two more manageable parts: learning the primary structure with the backbone and learning a small-norm correction with the residual experts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2. </span>Support for Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 2.3 (Efficacy of Guided Complementary Filtering as a Backbone). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> (Guided Complementary Filtering)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 2.3 (Efficacy of Guided Complementary Filtering as a Backbone). ‣ 2.2. Motivation ‣ 2. Preliminary ‣ Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> states that the Guided Complementary Filtering paradigm, guided by learned connectivity patterns, serves as a powerful and robust backbone for our architecture. This section provides the theoretical and empirical support for this claim. This principle is grounded in Graph Signal Processing (GSP) and the theory of perfect reconstruction in filter banks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Justification from Perfect Reconstruction.</span>
From a GSP perspective, node features <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a signal on the graph. A single graph filter, whether low-pass or high-pass, will inevitably lose information by attenuating certain frequency components of this signal. A more robust approach is to use a filter bank with complementary filters to decompose the signal, with the ideal goal of achieving <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">perfect reconstruction</span>—the ability to losslessly recover the original signal from the filtered outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ekambaram, 2013</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A classic example is the Simple Spline-like filter bank. For a <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph, its Low-Pass Filter (LPF) performs a weighted average, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{LP}=\frac{1}{2}(I+\frac{1}{d}A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{LP}=\frac{1}{2}(I+\frac{1}{d}A)</annotation></semantics></math>, while its High-Pass Filter (HPF) performs a weighted difference, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{HP}=\frac{1}{2}(I-\frac{1}{d}A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{HP}=\frac{1}{2}(I-\frac{1}{d}A)</annotation></semantics></math>. These operators are perfectly complementary, as their sum is the identity matrix, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{LP}+H_{HP}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{LP}+H_{HP}=I</annotation></semantics></math>, guaranteeing perfect reconstruction. The foundational experts in our backbone, SGC (an LPF) and LapSGC (an HPF), are designed based on this principle of complementarity. Although they do not strictly satisfy the perfect reconstruction property, their core function is to decompose the graph signal into two complementary low- and high-frequency channels, thereby capturing more comprehensive information than any single filter alone.</p>
</div>
